# v0.24.18 — Commit index since v0.23.0

## Fix — ZK proof generation blocked by systemd MemoryDenyWriteExecute

The `aitbc-coordinator-api` systemd unit used `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes`, which
prevents the Node.js V8 JIT from allocating read/write/execute memory pages.
snarkjs spawns a Node worker for Groth16 proof generation, so every call to
`/v1/zk/generate` crashed with the V8 fatal error
`Check failed: 12 == (*__errno_location())`.

Changed `MemoryDenyWriteExecute` to `no` in
`apps/coordinator-api/aitbc-coordinator-api.service` with a comment explaining
that snarkjs/Node requires RWX pages. Restarted `aitbc-coordinator-api` on
`hub.aitbc` and validated that `/v1/zk/generate` and end-to-end ZK-gated jobs
produce verified `receipt_model` proofs and release escrow.

## Fix — `aitbc-miner` default offer command uses `--service-type` options

The `aitbc market offer` command moved to option-style arguments
(`--service-type`, `--model-or-variant`, `--price`), but
`apps/miner/production_miner.py` still built the command with positional
arguments. This caused every default offer refresh on `aitbc3` to fail with
`Missing option '--service-type'`.

Updated `publish_default_offers()` to pass the canonical option flags, keeping
`--unit` and `--description` as before. With `MINER_WALLET_ADDRESS` exported as
`SHOP_WALLET_ADDRESS`, the miner now successfully publishes (or skips when
identical) the Whisper, FFmpeg, and Ollama default offers.

## Fix — `Escrow` DB lookups use the correct primary key

Three call sites in `apps/blockchain-node/src/aitbc_chain/rpc/escrow_routes.py`
used `session.get(Escrow, (job_id, chain_id))`, copying the composite-key
pattern from `Account`. The `Escrow` model has a single-column primary key on
`job_id`, so that identifier raised a SQLAlchemy primary-key-count error and
aborted the `session_scope()` before any row could be written or updated.

This caused escrow lock, release and refund operations to silently persist
nothing to the chain database ("Failed to persist escrow to DB after lock").

Updated the three sites to `session.get(Escrow, job_id)` to match the model and
the other four call sites in the same file.

## Fix — `PaymentService.create_payment` writes the amount back to the job

`Job.payment_amount` and `Job.payment_token` were only set in
`JobService.create_job` when the `JobCreate` request carried an amount. For the
common two-step path (job submitted first, then `POST /v1/payments`), the
payment record carried the real amount while the job row stayed `NULL`.

`miner.py`, `client.py` and `JobService.to_view()` all read the job row, so a
priced job could be treated as a zero-value job by the ZK/TEE threshold checks
and the refund sweeper, even though the payment was real.

Updated `PaymentService.create_payment` to set `job.payment_amount` and
`job.payment_token` from the `JobPaymentCreate` data, keeping the job and the
payment row authoritative. Added a regression test in
`apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_payments_refund.py`.
